(2922563, 402450, 14414, '<td valign="top">ahhh this was underrated (well, not so surprising tbh for a cat shot to be underrated), but its a cool shot nonetheless. I love the sharpness on the frizzy fur and then the softer legs, and the composition works really well with her coming right at you</td>', 'ahhh this was underrated (well, not so surprising tbh for a cat shot to be underrated), but its a cool shot nonetheless. I love the sharpness on the frizzy fur and then the softer legs, and the composition works really well with her coming right at you', '2006-10-12 14:18:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(2922997, 403598, 30861, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Hawk5000:</b></div><hr/><i> I shot this in Antibes France. It is my second time entering a challenge. I was several blocks from the focus point so I used my zoom lens at 220mm. I also wanted to use a small aperture f25 to increase the depth of field. With no tripod I probably should have used a larger aperture to get a sharper photo but I like the soft feel of it. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>VERY nice shot. For what it''s worth I think this is reasonably high contrast. And like melethia, I like the softness of it. However, you should be aware that stopping down to f/25 usually robs sharpness. It may increase DOF, and sometimes you have to do it, but very small apertures run afoul of something called the "circle of confusion": Google it to see discussions. In a nutshell, if you go past f/11 or so (it depends on the focal length of the lens) you begin to get diffraction that obliterates fine detail.<br/> <br/>R.</td>', 'Originally posted by Hawk5000: I shot this in Antibes France. It is my second time entering a challenge. I was several blocks from the focus point so I used my zoom lens at 220mm. I also wanted to use a small aperture f25 to increase the depth of field. With no tripod I probably should have used a larger aperture to get a sharper photo but I like the soft feel of it.   VERY nice shot. For what it''s worth I think this is reasonably high contrast. And like melethia, I like the softness of it. However, you should be aware that stopping down to f/25 usually robs sharpness. It may increase DOF, and sometimes you have to do it, but very small apertures run afoul of something called the "circle of confusion": Google it to see discussions. In a nutshell, if you go past f/11 or so (it depends on the focal length of the lens) you begin to get diffraction that obliterates fine detail. R.', '2006-10-12 16:11:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(2923012, 405162, 54406, '<td valign="top">your syle is so great. minimalism and negative space are my favorite things in photographs. way to go.</td>', 'your syle is so great. minimalism and negative space are my favorite things in photographs. way to go.', '2006-10-12 16:14:26.000000', NULL, 0),
