(2923359, 403598, 59458, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Bear_Music:</b></div><hr/><i> [quote=Hawk5000]<br/> <br/>VERY nice shot. For what it''s worth I think this is reasonably high contrast. And like melethia, I like the softness of it. However, you should be aware that stopping down to f/25 usually robs sharpness. It may increase DOF, and sometimes you have to do it, but very small apertures run afoul of something called the "circle of confusion": Google it to see discussions. In a nutshell, if you go past f/11 or so (it depends on the focal length of the lens) you begin to get diffraction that obliterates fine detail.<br/> <br/>R. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for the input. Very helpful.</td>', 'Originally posted by Bear_Music: [quote=Hawk5000] VERY nice shot. For what it''s worth I think this is reasonably high contrast. And like melethia, I like the softness of it. However, you should be aware that stopping down to f/25 usually robs sharpness. It may increase DOF, and sometimes you have to do it, but very small apertures run afoul of something called the "circle of confusion": Google it to see discussions. In a nutshell, if you go past f/11 or so (it depends on the focal length of the lens) you begin to get diffraction that obliterates fine detail. R.   Thanks for the input. Very helpful.', '2006-10-12 18:15:09.000000', NULL, 0),
